<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aceh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aceh is the northernmost province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, at the tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a>. From the 16th century it was the seat of the Aceh Sultanate, a leading Muslim power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, which resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a> from 1873. After decades of separatist conflict and the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> tsunami, a peace agreement in 2005 gave the province special autonomy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aceh.jpg" alt="Aceh" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aceh_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first evidence of human habitation in Aceh comes from a site near the Tamiang River, where shell middens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> suggest occupation from around 10,000 BCE. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> culture existed in the area. Aceh is thought to have been one of the earliest centres of Islam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> found Peureulak a Muslim town in 1292, and the gravestone of Sultan Malikussaleh, first Muslim ruler of Samudra, is dated 1297.</p><p>The Sultanate of Aceh was established by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mughayat-syah">Ali Mughayat Syah</a> in 1511, and in the early 17th century it was the wealthiest, most powerful and most cultivated state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial government declared war on Aceh on 26 March 1873, citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> operating from Aceh. The campaign quickly drove out the sultan, but local leaders fought on in about four decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war marked by high levels of atrocities.</p><p>Aceh was incorporated into North Sumatra in 1950, which led to the Acehnese Rebellion of 1953–59. In 1976 Hasan Muhammad di Tiro proclaimed independence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-aceh-movement">Free Aceh Movement</a> took up the struggle. On 26 December 2004 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> tsunami killed over 170,000 people in Aceh. It helped bring about the Helsinki Agreement of 15 August 2005, under which Aceh received special autonomy and the movement disarmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Aceh is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on the northern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a> as its capital and largest city. It is the only Indonesian province to officially integrate Islamic law. The Acehnese, the largest of ten indigenous groups, speak a language of the Aceh-Chamic group that was traditionally written in Arabic script. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 5,274,871 people. Gunung Leuser National Park protects the Sumatran tiger, orangutan and Sumatran elephant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aceh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four queens ruled <b>Aceh</b> in succession from 1641 to 1699, beginning with Taj ul-Alam Safiatuddin Syah, daughter of the conqueror Iskandar Muda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Counter-insurgency campaigns were fought in <b>Aceh</b> until 2005 and continue at a lower level in Papua.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese attacked its capitals repeatedly and destroyed Johor Lama in 1587; <b>Aceh</b> sacked Batu Sawar in 1613 and carried members of the royal family to Sumatra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in United Nations peacekeeping, and former president Martti Ahtisaari received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2008 for his mediation in Namibia, <b>Aceh</b> and Kosovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The fall of Malacca scattered the literary centres, as writers and scholars fled to places beyond the reach of European colonial powers, and new Malay works began to appear in <b>Aceh</b>, Java, Makassar, the Moluccas and Champa.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aceh</b> attacked repeatedly and finally conquered it in 1613 under Iskandar Muda.</blockquote><a href="/state/aru-kingdom">Aru Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
